==English==
{{wikipedia|lede (news)}}

===Alternative spellings===
* [[lead]]

===Etymology===
Mid-20th century neologism from a deliberate misspelling of {{term|lead}} (reverting to its archaic, phonetic spelling – cf. [[#Middle English|Middle English]] below), intended to avoid confusion with its [[homograph]] meaning a strip of type metal used for positioning type in the frame.<ref name=wotd>WOTD 2000</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lēd}}, {{IPA|/liːd/}}, {{SAMPA|/li:d/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{US|journalism|slang}} The [[introduction|introductory]] [[paragraph|paragraph(s)]] of a [[newspaper]] or other [[article]]. (Usage is “customary in many editorial rooms”<!--1982 quote on Citations page-->.){{fact}}

====Quotations====
* ''For examples of the use of this word see: [[Citations:lede|Citations]]''

====Usage notes====
Usage seems mostly confined to the U.S.<ref>Current citations are from U.S. sources. The only occurence found in 2008 on ''The Guardian'' website is made by the “editor of Guardian America”, saying “The lede (as we spell it) story in today’s NYT is ...” on his [http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/michaeltomasky/2008/oct/15/johnmccain-williamtimmons op/ed blog]. Other occurences on .co.uk sites all quote the lead/lede Wikipedia articles.</ref> Journalistic usage noted as “sometimes spelled” in 1959, “often spelled” in 1969, and asserted in the 1979 reprint of a 1974 book (cf. Citations page below). Jargon not listed in regular dictionaries.<ref name=Safire>Safire 1990: "You will not find this spelling in dictionaries; it is still an insiders' variant, steadily growing in frequency of use. [...] Will ''lede'' break out of its insider status and find its way into general use? [...] To suggest this is becoming standard would be misledeing<!--sic:misledeing-->"</ref><ref name=wotd/>

